  "live_records": {
    "q-116649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116649",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273315/assets/fcc9450e3721e1ed8e78846049443d848411e5001bd90749529db7867c868d82.png)\n\nA graphic designer is animating a circle in such a way that the area of the circle is decreasing at a rate of $12\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the circle is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $3\\,\\text{cm}.$",
      "worked_solution": "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=3,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=3\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (3)=6\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\pi $ and $C=6\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -12\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{12\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
